@# ------------------------------------- ENGLISH EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.english.event.group-discussion
  @scene school.english.event.group-discussion
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.english.event.reading-aloud
  @scene school.english.event.reading-aloud
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.english.event.surprise-quiz
  @scene school.english.event.surprise-quiz
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @when not daily.school_quiz_english
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.english.event.student-distraction
  @scene school.english.event.student-distraction
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.english.event.group-discussion -> @return
@choices "How do you participate?"

@passage opening

  The teacher closes the textbook and asks the class to discuss whether a narrator can be trustworthy while still being mistaken.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor turns slightly in your direction, clearly waiting to hear what you think.
  @else
  The room stays awkwardly quiet while everyone avoids being the first to answer.
  @endif

  @choice discussion-argue "Offer your own interpretation"
    @check grade english difficult

    @success -> .argued
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .misread
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice discussion-listen "Listen to the other answers" -> .listened
  @endchoice

  @choice discussion-taylor "Compare ideas with Taylor" -> .taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
    @change relationship taylor.friendship 1
  @endchoice

  @passage argued

  You explain that a narrator can report events honestly and still misunderstand what those events mean. The teacher nods and uses your answer to move the discussion forward.   @change grade english 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage misread

  You start confidently, but mix up the narrator's assumptions with the author's intent. The teacher corrects the distinction before moving the discussion on.  @change grade english -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage listened

  You keep quiet and follow the argument as it moves around the room. A few of the answers are surprisingly convincing.

  @next -> @return

  @passage taylor

  @random
  You and Taylor quietly compare interpretations before sharing a combined answer with the class.
  @or
  You and Taylor disagree on the meaning of the passage, but you both learn from the other's perspective.
  @or
  You and Taylor find that you have similar ideas, and you feel more confident about your own interpretation.
  @endrandom


  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.english.event.reading-aloud -> @return
  @choices "What do you do?"

  @passage opening

  The teacher asks for someone to read the next page aloud. It's some 
  @random
    dense prose from a classic novel.
    @or
    poetry that is difficult to read aloud.
    @or
    a passage that is full of unfamiliar phrasing.
  @endrandom

  @if player.education.english.grade >= 70
  You're pretty sure you've read this passage before, so you know what to expect.
  @else
  The unfamiliar phrasing makes the passage look more intimidating than it sounded a moment ago.
  @endif

  @choice reading-volunteer "Volunteer to read"
    @check skill resolve tricky

    @success -> .steady
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .stumbled
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice reading-follow "Follow along quietly" -> .followed
  @endchoice

  @choice reading-taylor "Draw attention to taylor" -> .taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
    @change relationship taylor.friendship -1
  @endchoice

  @passage steady

  You keep a measured pace and land the difficult sentences cleanly. By the end, several classmates are following your rhythm instead of the page.  @change grade english 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage stumbled

  A sentence catches you halfway through. You recover, but not before the room hears the hesitation.  @change grade english -1

  @if player.education.english.grade < 70
  You feel embarrassed, this shouldn't have been so difficult!
  @endif

  @next  -> @return

  @passage followed

  Someone else volunteers. You follow the page and make a note beside the phrases the teacher emphasises.

  @next -> @return

  @passage taylor
  Before Taylor can protest you grab {{npc.taylor.dependent}} arm and raise it. 

  "W-what are you doing?!" Taylor whispers, but you ignore {{npc.taylor.dependent}} and draw the teacher's attention.

  The teacher calls on {{npc.taylor.object}} and Taylors stammers through the passage. You feel a little guilty, but you also feel a little smug.
  @next -> @return

@endsequence

@sequence school.english.event.surprise-quiz -> @return
  @choices "Choose an answer"
  @onenter
    @effect daily-flag school_quiz_english true
  @endonenter
  @system school.quiz {"bank":"english.core","questions":3}
@endsequence

@sequence school.english.event.student-distraction -> @return
  @choices "What do you do?"

  @passage opening

  A student in the back of the room is making a racket, and the teacher is having trouble keeping the class focused.

  @choice distraction-ignore "Ignore it" -> .ignored
  @endchoice

  @choice distraction-help "Tell the student to shut up"
    @check skill resolve tricky

    @success -> .helped-success
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .helped-failure
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @passage ignored

  You keep your head down and focus on your work. It's very hard to focus. 
  The teacher eventually regains control of the class.  @change grade english -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage helped-success

  You raise your hand and politely ask the student to quiet down. The student complies, and the teacher thanks you for your help.
  @#change relationship teacher 1
  @change grade english 2
  @next -> @return

  @passage helped-failure

  You try to get the student's attention, but they ignore you and continue to be disruptive. The teacher has to step in and reprimand them.
  @#change relationship teacher -1
  @change grade english -1

  @next -> @return

@endsequence

@# ------------------------------------- MATH EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.math.event.problem-solving
  @scene school.math.event.problem-solving
  @pool school.class.math
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "math"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.math.event.group-work
  @scene school.math.event.group-work
  @pool school.class.math
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "math"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.math.event.surprise-quiz
  @scene school.math.event.surprise-quiz
  @pool school.class.math
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "math"
  @when not daily.school_quiz_math
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.math.event.called-to-answer
  @scene school.math.event.called-to-answer
  @pool school.class.math
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "math"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.math.event.problem-solving -> @return
  @choices "How do you approach the problem?"

  @passage opening

  The teacher presents a challenging problem on the board and asks the class to solve it.

  @choice problem-solve "Raise your hand"
    @check grade math tricky

    @success -> .solved
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .struggled
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice problem-listen "Stay quiet" -> .listened
  @endchoice

  @passage solved
  You confidently solve the problem and explain your reasoning to the class. The teacher praises your work.  @change grade math 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage struggled
  You attempt to solve the problem, but you make a mistake in your calculations. The teacher corrects you and explains the right approach.  @change grade math -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage listened
  You stay quiet and listen to the other students' solutions. You learn a few new techniques, but you don't get to participate in the discussion.  @change grade math 1
  
  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.math.event.group-work -> @return
  @choices "How do you contribute?"

  @passage opening

  The teacher divides the class into small groups to work on a set of problems together.

  @choice group-contribute "Share your ideas"
    @check skill resolve tricky

    @success -> .contributed
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .ignored
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice group-listen "Listen to your group members" -> .listened
  @endchoice

  @passage contributed
  You actively participate in the group discussion and help solve the problems. Your group appreciates your input.  @change grade math 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage ignored
  You try to contribute, but your group members don't seem to value your input. You feel a bit left out.  @change grade math -1  @change stat stress 5

  @next -> @return

  @passage listened
  You stay quiet and listen to your group members' ideas. You learn a lot, but you don't get to share your own thoughts.  @change grade math 1
  
  @next -> @return

@endsequence

@sequence school.math.event.surprise-quiz -> @return
  @choices "Choose an answer"
  @onenter
    @effect daily-flag school_quiz_math true
  @endonenter
  @system school.quiz {"bank":"math.core","questions":3}
@endsequence

@sequence school.math.event.called-to-answer -> @return
  @choices "How do you respond?"

  @passage opening

  The teacher calls on you to answer a question on the board.

  @choice answer-correct "Answer confidently"
    @when player.education.math.grade >= 70
    @check grade math easy 
    @success -> .correct
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .incorrect
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice answer-incorrect "Guess"
    @check grade math difficult
    @success -> .correct
    @endsuccess

    @failure -> .incorrect
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice answer-ask "Stay at your seat." -> .failure
    @change grade math -1
  @endchoice

  @passage correct
  You confidently solve the problem and explain your reasoning. The teacher praises your work.  @change grade math 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage incorrect
  You make a mistake in your calculations and the teacher corrects you. You feel embarrassed, but you learn from the experience.  @change grade math -2

   @next -> @return
  
  @passage failure
  You stay at your seat, hoping the teacher will call on someone else. The teacher eventually gives up and moves on, but you feel a bit embarrassed for not participating.
  

  @next -> @return

@endsequence


@# ------------------------------------- HISTORY EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.history.event.mock-trial
  @scene school.history.event.mock-trial
  @pool school.class.history
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "history"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.history.event.primary-source
  @scene school.history.event.primary-source
  @pool school.class.history
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "history"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.history.event.crisis-cabinet
  @scene school.history.event.crisis-cabinet
  @pool school.class.history
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "history"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.history.event.disputed-map
  @scene school.history.event.disputed-map
  @pool school.class.history
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "history"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.history.event.mock-trial -> @return
  @choices "Choose your role"

  @passage opening

  The teacher turns the classroom into a mock tribunal. Your group must judge whether a ruler should be remembered as a reformer or a tyrant.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor gathers the group's notes and asks which side you want to argue.
  @endif

  @choice trial-defend "Argue that the ruler was a reformer"
    @check grade history tricky
    @success -> .defense-strong
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .defense-weak
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice trial-prosecute "Argue that the ruler was a tyrant"
    @check skill speech tricky
    @success -> .prosecution-strong
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .prosecution-weak
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice trial-partner "Build the case with Taylor" -> .partner
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage defense-strong

  You connect the ruler's reforms to conditions people actually faced at the time. Even the opposing side has trouble finding a clean rebuttal.  @change grade history 2

  @choice trial-share-credit "Share the closing argument with Taylor" -> .shared-verdict
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @choice trial-close-alone "Deliver the closing argument yourself" -> .solo-verdict
  @endchoice

  @passage defense-weak

  You lean too heavily on one reform and overlook the people harmed by it. The opposing side immediately points out the gap.  @change grade history -1  @change stat stress 2

  @choice trial-admit-gap "Acknowledge the flaw and revise your argument" -> .honest-recovery
  @endchoice

  @choice trial-bluff "Bluff your way through the objection"
    @check skill resolve difficult
    @success -> .solo-verdict
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .failed-verdict
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @passage prosecution-strong

  You arrange the evidence into a sharp argument about power, fear, and the cost of the ruler's victories. The room goes quiet during your conclusion.  @change grade history 2  @change skill speech 0.1

  @next -> .solo-verdict

  @passage prosecution-weak

  Your accusation sounds forceful, but the chronology does not support it. The teacher stops you to correct two of the dates.  @change grade history -1  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> .failed-verdict

  @passage partner

  You and Taylor divide the evidence. Taylor spots a contradiction in a witness account while you connect it to the wider political situation.

  @choice trial-follow-taylor "Let Taylor present the contradiction" -> .shared-verdict
    @change relationship taylor.friendship 2
  @endchoice

  @choice trial-take-point "Use Taylor's discovery in your own argument" -> .solo-verdict
    @change relationship taylor.friendship -1
  @endchoice

  @passage honest-recovery

  You concede the weak point and rebuild the argument around stronger evidence. The teacher praises the correction more than the original claim.  @change grade history 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage shared-verdict

  You and Taylor deliver the conclusion together. Your case does not answer every question, but it is balanced and convincing.  @change grade history 1  @change relationship taylor.friendship 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage solo-verdict

  You deliver the final argument yourself. The teacher nods at the structure of your case before announcing the class verdict.  @change skill speech 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage failed-verdict

  The argument falls apart under questioning. You return to your seat with a much clearer idea of which evidence you should have used.  @change grade history -1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.history.event.primary-source -> @return
  @choices "Which account do you trust?"

  @passage opening

  The teacher distributes two accounts of the same protest: an official report calling it a riot and a private diary describing a peaceful march.

  @choice source-inspect "Inspect both accounts for inconsistencies"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .clue-found
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .clue-missed
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice source-official "Trust the official report" -> .official
  @endchoice

  @choice source-taylor "Compare notes with Taylor" -> .taylor-notes
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage clue-found

  You notice that the report was filed before the time it claims the protest ended. Someone completed the paperwork before events had finished.  @change skill perception 0.1

  @choice source-expose "Explain why the report is unreliable" -> .exposed
  @endchoice

  @choice source-ask-taylor "Quietly show the timestamp to Taylor" -> .shared-clue
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage clue-missed

  The accounts contradict one another, but you cannot find enough context to decide why.  @change grade history -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage official

  You argue that the official document is more dependable because it was written for the public record. The teacher asks who controlled that record and why.  @change stat mind 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage taylor-notes

  Taylor points to the language each author uses. Together, you separate what both accounts agree happened from the motives each one assigns.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 1  @change grade history 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage exposed

  You present the timestamp and explain that neither account can be accepted without checking when and why it was produced.  @change grade history 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage shared-clue

  Taylor's eyes widen when she sees the timestamp. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} encourages you to present it, then backs up your explanation when the teacher questions it.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade history 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.history.event.crisis-cabinet -> @return
  @choices "Advise the cabinet"

  @passage opening

  The class role-plays a government facing a blockade. Every group has limited food, an unreliable ally, and only minutes to recommend a response.

  @choice cabinet-negotiate "Recommend immediate negotiations"
    @check skill speech difficult
    @success -> .negotiation-success
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .negotiation-failure
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice cabinet-prepare "Prepare defenses while gathering information" -> .cautious
  @endchoice

  @choice cabinet-support-taylor "Support Taylor's risky proposal" -> .taylor-plan
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage negotiation-success

  You frame negotiation as a way to divide the opposing alliance rather than surrender. Several undecided students switch to your side.  @change grade history 2  @change skill speech 0.1

  @next -> .consequences

  @passage negotiation-failure

  Your proposal sounds reasonable, but you cannot answer what happens if the blockade continues during the talks.  @change grade history -1  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> .consequences

  @passage cautious

  You refuse to commit until the group verifies its reports. The decision costs time, but it prevents the class from acting on false information.  @change grade history 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> .consequences

  @passage taylor-plan

  Taylor proposes sending a secret convoy through the blockade. You help {{npc.taylor.object}} answer the group's objections and refine the route.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2

  @next -> .consequences

  @passage consequences

  The teacher reveals the historical outcome. Every available option carried a cost, and the class spends the final minutes comparing those costs with its own decisions.  @change stat mind 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.history.event.disputed-map -> @return
  @choices "Interpret the map"

  @passage opening

  A faded map on the projector shows three borders for the same territory, each drawn by a different government.

  @choice map-date "Work out which border was drawn first"
    @check grade history tricky
    @success -> .map-solved
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .map-confused
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice map-question "Question whether any border is neutral" -> .map-critical
  @endchoice

  @choice map-taylor "Ask Taylor to trace the border changes with you" -> .map-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage map-solved

  You match the place names to the period when each language was used. The layers suddenly form a clear chronology.  @change grade history 2  @change skill perception 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage map-confused

  You mistake a later occupation line for the original border. The teacher corrects the sequence and explains why the map was designed to encourage that reading.  @change grade history -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage map-critical

  You point out that every version places its preferred border in the strongest colour. The teacher uses your observation to discuss maps as political arguments.  @change grade history 1  @change stat mind 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage map-taylor

  You trace the borders while Taylor reads the legend. When your interpretations differ, you combine them into a stronger explanation.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 1  @change grade history 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@# ------------------------------------- SCIENCE EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.science.event.runaway-reaction
  @scene school.science.event.runaway-reaction
  @pool school.class.science
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "science"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.science.event.mystery-sample
  @scene school.science.event.mystery-sample
  @pool school.class.science
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "science"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.science.event.bridge-challenge
  @scene school.science.event.bridge-challenge
  @pool school.class.science
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "science"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.science.event.impossible-result
  @scene school.science.event.impossible-result
  @pool school.class.science
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "science"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.science.event.runaway-reaction -> @return
  @choices "Respond to the reaction"

  @passage opening

  Halfway through a reaction, the liquid in your beaker changes colour too quickly. Foam climbs toward the rim while the instructions insist nothing should happen yet.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor freezes with the next reagent still in {{npc.taylor.dependent}} hand.
  @endif

  @choice reaction-shut-down "Shut the experiment down immediately"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .contained
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .spill
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice reaction-call-teacher "Call the teacher over" -> .reported
  @endchoice

  @choice reaction-taylor "Tell Taylor how to stabilize it" -> .taylor-stabilizes
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage contained

  You remove the heat and slide the beaker into the safety tray before the foam reaches the top. A mislabeled measuring spoon turns out to be the cause.  @change grade science 2  @change skill perception 0.1

  @choice reaction-report-cause "Show the mislabeled spoon to the teacher" -> .commended
  @endchoice

  @choice reaction-hide-cause "Quietly replace the spoon" -> .hidden
  @endchoice

  @passage spill

  You grab the wrong clamp. The beaker tips, covering the bench in harmless but foul-smelling foam.  @change grade science -1  @change stat hygiene -5  @change stat stress 3

  @next -> @return

  @passage reported

  The teacher cuts the heat and contains the reaction. You lose the experiment, but your quick report prevents the rest of the table from being splashed.  @change grade science 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage taylor-stabilizes

  Taylor follows your instructions while you monitor the foam. Together you bring the reaction under control just before it spills.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade science 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage commended

  The teacher thanks you for identifying an equipment problem and replaces the entire set before another group uses it.  @change grade science 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage hidden

  Nobody notices the substitution, but you spend the rest of the lesson wondering whether another group received faulty equipment too.  @change stat stress 2  @change grade science -1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.science.event.mystery-sample -> @return
  @choices "Examine the sample"

  @passage opening

  Each table receives an unlabeled mineral sample. The class must identify it without damaging it or asking the teacher for clues.

  @choice sample-observe "Study its colour, texture, and fracture lines"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .sample-clues
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .sample-misread
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice sample-test "Perform every permitted test" -> .sample-methodical
  @endchoice

  @choice sample-taylor "Divide the tests with Taylor" -> .sample-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage sample-clues

  Tiny cubic breaks along one edge narrow the possibilities to two minerals. One final hardness test will settle it.  @change skill perception 0.1

  @choice sample-careful-test "Perform a careful hardness test" -> .sample-correct
  @endchoice

  @choice sample-guess "Trust your first impression" -> .sample-guess
  @endchoice

  @passage sample-misread

  Reflected light makes the sample seem metallic. You identify it confidently, only to learn that you were looking at residue from its container.  @change grade science -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage sample-methodical

  You work through the test sheet in order and eliminate each alternative. It takes longer, but your conclusion is difficult to dispute.  @change grade science 2  @change stat mind 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage sample-taylor

  Taylor records density while you test hardness. Your independent results point to the same mineral.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 1  @change grade science 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage sample-correct

  The sample scratches the reference plate exactly as predicted. Your identification is correct.  @change grade science 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage sample-guess

  Your first impression was close, but the teacher reveals a similar-looking mineral with a different hardness.  @change grade science -1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.science.event.bridge-challenge -> @return
  @choices "Build the bridge"

  @passage opening

  The teacher gives every group paper, tape, and ten minutes to build a bridge capable of holding a stack of textbooks.

  @choice bridge-design "Design a folded support structure"
    @check grade science difficult
    @success -> .bridge-strong
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .bridge-flawed
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice bridge-prototype "Build and test small prototypes" -> .bridge-tested
  @endchoice

  @choice bridge-taylor "Let Taylor design while you assemble" -> .bridge-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage bridge-strong

  Your triangular folds distribute the load across the entire span. The bridge survives every textbook the teacher places on it.  @change grade science 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage bridge-flawed

  The centre support bends sideways under the second book. Once you see the failure, the missing brace is painfully obvious.  @change grade science -1  @change stat stress 2

  @choice bridge-salvage "Reinforce the weak side before the final test"
    @check skill resolve tricky
    @success -> .bridge-salvaged
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .bridge-collapsed
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice bridge-accept "Accept the failed design" -> .bridge-collapsed
  @endchoice

  @passage bridge-tested

  Several ugly prototypes fail, but each teaches you where the paper bends. Your final bridge is inelegant and remarkably strong.  @change grade science 1  @change skill perception 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage bridge-taylor

  Taylor sketches a daring arch while you translate it into careful folds. The finished bridge sways under load but holds.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade science 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage bridge-salvaged

  You add a diagonal brace seconds before testing. The repaired bridge holds long enough to earn a passing result.  @change grade science 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage bridge-collapsed

  The bridge folds flat beneath the books. At least the collapse demonstrates exactly where the forces were concentrated.

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.science.event.impossible-result -> @return
  @choices "Handle the result"

  @passage opening

  Your measurements imply that the test object gained mass during a closed experiment. The result contradicts both the lesson and common sense.

  @choice result-repeat "Repeat the experiment from the beginning"
    @check grade science tricky
    @success -> .result-explained
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .result-persists
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice result-adjust "Adjust the suspicious measurement" -> .result-fudged
  @endchoice

  @choice result-taylor "Ask Taylor to audit your notes" -> .result-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage result-explained

  On the second run, you notice condensation collecting beneath the lid before weighing. The supposedly closed experiment was trapping water from the air.  @change grade science 2  @change stat mind 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage result-persists

  The strange result appears again. You document every step and submit it without pretending you understand the cause.  @change grade science 1  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage result-fudged

  You alter the number until it resembles the expected result. The teacher spots that your raw notes and graph no longer match.  @change grade science -2  @change stat stress 3

  @next -> @return

  @passage result-taylor

  Taylor finds a unit conversion copied incorrectly from the board. Correcting it makes every measurement fall into place.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade science 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@# ------------------------------------- ART EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.art.event.partner-portrait
  @scene school.art.event.partner-portrait
  @pool school.class.art
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "art"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.art.event.critique-circle
  @scene school.art.event.critique-circle
  @pool school.class.art
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "art"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.art.event.shared-mural
  @scene school.art.event.shared-mural
  @pool school.class.art
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "art"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.art.event.ruined-artwork
  @scene school.art.event.ruined-artwork
  @pool school.class.art
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "art"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.art.event.partner-portrait -> @return
  @choices "Choose your approach"

  @passage opening

  The teacher pairs students for quick portraits. You must capture an expression rather than simply copy a face.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor takes the seat opposite you and tries to hold a serious pose, though the corners of {{npc.taylor.dependent}} mouth keep twitching.
  @else
  A student from the next table sits opposite you and stares past your shoulder with determined stillness.
  @endif

  @choice portrait-observe "Study the details before drawing"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .portrait-honest
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .portrait-stiff
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice portrait-flatter "Make the portrait deliberately flattering" -> .portrait-flattering
  @endchoice

  @choice portrait-caricature "Draw a playful caricature" -> .portrait-caricature
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage portrait-honest

  You stop thinking in terms of individual features and capture the expression holding them together. The portrait feels recognizably alive.  @change grade art 2  @change skill perception 0.1

  @choice portrait-show-private "Show Taylor privately first" -> .portrait-private
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @choice portrait-display "Place it on the class display" -> .portrait-display
  @endchoice

  @passage portrait-stiff

  Every feature is technically present, yet the face looks posed and distant. The teacher suggests drawing the movement of the expression instead of its outline.  @change grade art -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage portrait-flattering

  You smooth every awkward line and exaggerate the most appealing features. The result is attractive, although it resembles an advertisement more than a person.  @change grade art 1

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor seems pleased at first, then studies the drawing as if wondering what you chose not to include.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 1
  @endif

  @next -> @return

  @passage portrait-caricature

  You exaggerate Taylor's barely contained grin until it takes over the entire page.

  @choice portrait-reveal-joke "Reveal it as a joke" -> .caricature-reveal
  @endchoice

  @choice portrait-hide-joke "Hide it and start over" -> .caricature-hidden
  @endchoice

  @passage portrait-private

  Taylor studies the portrait quietly before smiling. "You make me look like I'm thinking something clever," {{npc.taylor.subject}} says.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage portrait-display

  The teacher points out your use of expression and asks the class what story they imagine behind it.  @change grade art 1  @change stat stress 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage caricature-reveal

  Taylor stares at the enormous grin, then breaks the pose by laughing. The teacher calls it expressive, if not especially subtle.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage caricature-hidden

  Taylor catches a glimpse before you cover the page. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} spends the rest of the exercise trying to see what you drew.  @change relationship taylor.friendship -1  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.art.event.critique-circle -> @return
  @choices "Respond to the critique"

  @passage opening

  Your latest piece is pinned to the board for a class critique. The first comments are polite, but one student calls the composition confused.

  @choice critique-explain "Explain what you intended"
    @check skill speech tricky
    @success -> .critique-explained
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .critique-defensive
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice critique-listen "Listen without defending it" -> .critique-listened
  @endchoice

  @choice critique-taylor "Ask Taylor for an honest opinion" -> .critique-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage critique-explained

  You explain the visual path you wanted the viewer to follow. Once you name it, the class offers practical ways to make that path clearer.  @change grade art 2  @change skill speech 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage critique-defensive

  Your explanation turns into an argument about why the class misunderstood you. The teacher reminds you that confusion is itself useful feedback.  @change grade art -1  @change stat stress 3

  @choice critique-apologize "Back down and ask for specific advice" -> .critique-recovered
  @endchoice

  @choice critique-double-down "Insist that nothing needs changing" -> .critique-stubborn
  @endchoice

  @passage critique-listened

  You write down the comments instead of answering them. Some criticism feels unfair, but several people identify the same weak area.  @change grade art 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage critique-taylor

  Taylor hesitates, then points to the strongest part of the piece and explains why the rest distracts from it.

  @choice critique-thank-taylor "Thank Taylor for being direct" -> .critique-taylor-thanks
  @endchoice

  @choice critique-dismiss-taylor "Dismiss Taylor's criticism" -> .critique-taylor-dismissed
  @endchoice

  @passage critique-recovered

  You take a breath and ask the class where their eyes go first. The answers give you a clear revision plan.  @change grade art 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage critique-stubborn

  The discussion ends awkwardly. Your work remains unchanged, along with the problem everyone noticed.  @change grade art -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage critique-taylor-thanks

  You mark Taylor's suggestion beside your sketch. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} looks relieved that you took the honesty well.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage critique-taylor-dismissed

  Taylor shrugs and stops offering suggestions. A few minutes later, the teacher identifies the same problem.  @change relationship taylor.friendship -2  @change grade art -1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.art.event.shared-mural -> @return
  @choices "Contribute to the mural"

  @passage opening

  The class must turn a blank paper wall into one connected cityscape. Every table has a different idea about where its section should lead.

  @choice mural-coordinate "Coordinate the competing designs"
    @check skill speech difficult
    @success -> .mural-unified
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .mural-argument
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice mural-own-section "Focus entirely on your own section" -> .mural-independent
  @endchoice

  @choice mural-taylor "Develop Taylor's idea together" -> .mural-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage mural-unified

  You persuade each table to repeat one colour and one horizon line. The wildly different neighborhoods begin to look like parts of the same city.  @change grade art 2  @change skill speech 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage mural-argument

  Everyone agrees that the mural needs a plan, but nobody agrees that yours should be it. The discussion consumes most of the painting time.  @change stat stress 3  @change grade art -1

  @choice mural-compromise "Abandon your plan and find a compromise" -> .mural-compromise
  @endchoice

  @choice mural-take-over "Paint over the disputed boundary yourself" -> .mural-takeover
  @endchoice

  @passage mural-independent

  Your section is carefully rendered and visually strong, but it meets the neighboring sections in abrupt walls of mismatched colour.  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage mural-taylor

  Taylor proposes turning the mismatched borders into a river winding through the city. You extend the idea across both sections.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage mural-compromise

  You reduce the plan to a shared horizon and let every table interpret the details. It is enough to hold the mural together.  @change grade art 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage mural-takeover

  The boundary looks cleaner after you repaint it, but the neighboring group is furious that part of its work disappeared.  @change grade art -1  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.art.event.ruined-artwork -> @return
  @choices "React to the accident"

  @passage opening

  A jar of cloudy paint water tips across a nearly finished drawing. Dark streams spread through the paper before anyone can lift it from the table.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  It is Taylor's drawing, and {{npc.taylor.subject}} stares at the spreading stain in horror.
  @else
  The student beside you stares at the spreading stain in horror.
  @endif

  @choice ruined-salvage "Try to turn the stain into part of the piece"
    @check grade art difficult
    @success -> .ruined-transformed
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .ruined-worse
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice ruined-blot "Blot the water before it spreads" -> .ruined-contained
  @endchoice

  @choice ruined-comfort "Reassure Taylor and offer your materials" -> .ruined-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage ruined-transformed

  You tilt the paper and guide the stain through the background. What looked like damage becomes a storm behind the original subject.  @change grade art 2

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor looks from the altered drawing to you with astonished relief.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2
  @endif

  @next -> @return

  @passage ruined-worse

  Your added marks muddy the remaining clean areas. The teacher finally separates the soaked page from the table and suggests starting again.  @change grade art -1  @change stat stress 3

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor tries not to look annoyed, without much success.  @change relationship taylor.friendship -1
  @endif

  @next -> @return

  @passage ruined-contained

  You sacrifice a stack of paper towels to stop the water. The drawing is scarred along one edge, but most of it survives.  @change skill perception 0.1  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage ruined-taylor

  You give Taylor your clean paper and help reconstruct the composition from memory. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} relaxes once the new version begins taking shape.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade art 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@# ------------------------------------- PHYSICAL EDUCATION EVENTS -------------------------------------

@entry school.physical-education.event.dodgeball
  @scene school.physical-education.event.dodgeball
  @pool school.class.physical-education
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "physical_education"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.physical-education.event.obstacle-course
  @scene school.physical-education.event.obstacle-course
  @pool school.class.physical-education
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "physical_education"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.physical-education.event.relay-race
  @scene school.physical-education.event.relay-race
  @pool school.class.physical-education
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "physical_education"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.physical-education.event.dangerous-player
  @scene school.physical-education.event.dangerous-player
  @pool school.class.physical-education
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "physical_education"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.physical-education.event.dodgeball -> @return
  @choices "Play the round"

  @passage opening

  The teacher divides the class for dodgeball. The opening whistle turns the gym into a storm of bouncing balls and shouted warnings.

  @choice dodgeball-aggressive "Push forward and play aggressively"
    @check skill fitness tricky
    @success -> .dodgeball-dominant
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .dodgeball-out
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice dodgeball-defensive "Stay back and watch for openings"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .dodgeball-catch
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .dodgeball-out
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice dodgeball-cover-taylor "Keep Taylor out of the crossfire" -> .dodgeball-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage dodgeball-dominant

  You dodge the first throw, recover the loose ball, and eliminate two opponents before they can retreat.  @change grade physical_education 2  @change skill fitness 0.1  @change stat energy -2

  @choice dodgeball-finish "Take the difficult final shot"
    @check skill perception difficult
    @success -> .dodgeball-win
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .dodgeball-overreach
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice dodgeball-pass "Pass to a better-positioned teammate" -> .dodgeball-team-win
  @endchoice

  @passage dodgeball-catch

  You read a throw early and catch it cleanly, bringing one of your teammates back into the game.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change skill perception 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-out

  You react a moment too late. The ball clips your leg, and you join the eliminated players against the wall.  @change grade physical_education -1  @change stat stress 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-taylor

  You call out incoming throws and pull Taylor behind cover at the right moments. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} survives the round much longer than expected.

  @choice dodgeball-taylor-sacrifice "Take a hit meant for Taylor" -> .dodgeball-sacrifice
  @endchoice

  @choice dodgeball-taylor-push "Push Taylor to make the winning throw" -> .dodgeball-taylor-shot
  @endchoice

  @passage dodgeball-win

  Your final throw catches the last opponent moving the wrong way. Your team erupts in celebration.  @change grade physical_education 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-overreach

  You throw before setting your feet. The ball sails wide, and the return throw catches you immediately.  @change stat stress 2

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-team-win

  Your teammate lands the easier shot. You do not get the dramatic finish, but the team wins because you passed.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-sacrifice

  The ball strikes your shoulder instead of Taylor. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} looks guilty until you point out that {{npc.taylor.subject }} is still in the game.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change stat health -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage dodgeball-taylor-shot

  Taylor hesitates, throws, and catches the final opponent on the ankle. {{npc.taylor.subject | cap}} turns to you in delighted disbelief.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade physical_education 1

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.physical-education.event.obstacle-course -> @return
  @choices "Approach the course"

  @passage opening

  Cones, vaulting boxes, ropes, and balance benches form a timed obstacle course across the gym.

  @if npc.taylor.present
  You and Taylor are assigned as a pair. Your time only counts once both of you cross the line.
  @else
  You are paired with another student, and your time only counts once both of you cross the line.
  @endif

  @choice obstacle-sprint "Set a fast pace from the start"
    @check skill endurance difficult
    @success -> .obstacle-fast
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .obstacle-exhausted
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice obstacle-steady "Use a steady, controlled pace" -> .obstacle-steady
  @endchoice

  @choice obstacle-taylor "Let Taylor set the pace" -> .obstacle-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage obstacle-fast

  You clear the opening barriers without breaking stride and reach the rope with time to spare.  @change grade physical_education 2  @change skill endurance 0.1  @change stat energy -3

  @next -> .obstacle-final

  @passage obstacle-exhausted

  You attack the first obstacles too quickly. By the rope climb, your arms are shaking and your partner has caught up with you.  @change stat energy -5  @change grade physical_education -1

  @choice obstacle-force "Force yourself through the final obstacles"
    @check skill resolve tricky
    @success -> .obstacle-recovered
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .obstacle-finished-late
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice obstacle-slow "Slow down and finish safely" -> .obstacle-finished-late
  @endchoice

  @passage obstacle-steady

  You sacrifice a few seconds to place every step securely. Neither you nor your partner needs to repeat an obstacle.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change skill fitness 0.1

  @next -> .obstacle-final

  @passage obstacle-taylor

  Taylor starts cautiously, then gains confidence when you match {{npc.taylor.dependent}} rhythm instead of rushing ahead.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2

  @next -> .obstacle-final

  @passage obstacle-final

  At the final balance bench, your partner slips and catches the edge with one foot.

  @choice obstacle-help "Stop and help your partner regain balance" -> .obstacle-helped
  @endchoice

  @choice obstacle-finish "Cross the line and call encouragement" -> .obstacle-solo-finish
  @endchoice

  @passage obstacle-recovered

  You find enough strength to complete the course without skipping an obstacle. The time is mediocre, but the recovery impresses the teacher.  @change skill resolve 0.1  @change grade physical_education 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage obstacle-finished-late

  You finish well outside the target time, exhausted but uninjured.  @change stat stress 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage obstacle-helped

  You step back onto the bench and offer a hand. Both of you cross together a few seconds later.  @change grade physical_education 1

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor squeezes your hand before letting go.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2
  @endif

  @next -> @return

  @passage obstacle-solo-finish

  You preserve your own time, but your pair's result does not count until your partner finishes.  @change grade physical_education -1

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor crosses without looking at you.  @change relationship taylor.friendship -1
  @endif

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.physical-education.event.relay-race -> @return
  @choices "Run your relay leg"

  @passage opening

  The class lines up for a relay. You are running the final leg, where a clean handoff matters as much as raw speed.

  @choice relay-safe "Prioritize a clean handoff"
    @check skill perception tricky
    @success -> .relay-clean
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .relay-dropped
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice relay-sprint "Start accelerating before the baton arrives"
    @check skill fitness difficult
    @success -> .relay-flying
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .relay-dropped
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice relay-taylor "Coordinate the handoff with Taylor" -> .relay-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @passage relay-clean

  The baton lands firmly in your palm. You enter the final straight close behind the leading runner.  @change skill perception 0.1

  @choice relay-chase "Spend everything trying to overtake"
    @check skill endurance tricky
    @success -> .relay-win
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .relay-second
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice relay-hold "Protect your position" -> .relay-second
  @endchoice

  @passage relay-flying

  You match the baton perfectly while already at speed. The other teams are still completing their handoffs when you hit the straight.  @change grade physical_education 2  @change skill fitness 0.1

  @next -> .relay-win

  @passage relay-dropped

  The baton strikes your fingertips and bounces onto the floor. Several teams pass while you turn back for it.  @change grade physical_education -1  @change stat stress 3

  @choice relay-recover "Recover the baton and finish the race" -> .relay-recovery
  @endchoice

  @choice relay-blame "Blame the incoming runner" -> .relay-blame
  @endchoice

  @passage relay-taylor

  You and Taylor rehearse the signal under your breath. When {{npc.taylor.subject}} calls it, the handoff feels effortless.  @change relationship taylor.friendship 2  @change grade physical_education 1

  @next -> .relay-clean

  @passage relay-win

  You lean through the finish just ahead of the nearest runner. Your legs burn, but your team wins.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change skill endurance 0.1  @change stat energy -4

  @next -> @return

  @passage relay-second

  You hold your form to the line and secure second place without fading at the end.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change stat energy -2

  @next -> @return

  @passage relay-recovery

  You grab the baton and finish despite having no chance to win. The teacher praises you for completing the race.  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage relay-blame

  The argument continues after the race. The teacher makes both of you repeat handoff drills while everyone else rests.  @change stat stress 2  @change grade physical_education -1

  @if npc.taylor.present
  Taylor is unimpressed by how quickly you assigned blame.  @change relationship taylor.friendship -1
  @endif

  @next -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.physical-education.event.dangerous-player -> @return
  @choices "Handle the rough play"

  @passage opening

  During a small team game, one student keeps making reckless challenges long after the teacher warns everyone to calm down.

  @choice rough-confront "Confront the student directly"
    @check skill resolve difficult
    @success -> .rough-backed-down
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .rough-escalates
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @choice rough-teacher "Call the teacher's attention to it" -> .rough-reported
  @endchoice

  @choice rough-protect-taylor "Stay close enough to protect Taylor" -> .rough-taylor
    @when npc.taylor.present
  @endchoice

  @choice rough-retaliate "Beat the student at the same game"
    @check skill strength difficult
    @success -> .rough-retaliation-success
    @endsuccess
    @failure -> .rough-retaliation-failure
    @endfailure
  @endchoice

  @passage rough-backed-down

  You tell the student plainly that the next reckless challenge will stop the game. The rest of the team supports you, and the behavior finally ends.  @change grade physical_education 1  @change skill resolve 0.1

  @next -> @return

  @passage rough-escalates

  The student laughs off the warning and shoulders past you on the next play. The teacher stops the game before the argument becomes a fight.  @change stat stress 3  @change grade physical_education -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage rough-reported

  The teacher watches the next play closely and removes the student after another dangerous challenge. The game becomes noticeably calmer.  @change grade physical_education 1

  @next -> @return

  @passage rough-taylor

  You warn Taylor whenever the student approaches and position yourself between them during contested plays.

  @choice rough-check-taylor "Ask whether Taylor wants to leave the game" -> .rough-taylor-cared
  @endchoice

  @choice rough-challenge-player "Challenge the student away from Taylor" -> .rough-taylor-risk
  @endchoice

  @passage rough-retaliation-success

  You meet the student's next challenge and hold your ground. The point is made, but the teacher penalizes both of you.  @change skill strength 0.1  @change grade physical_education -1  @change stat health -1

  @next -> @return

  @passage rough-retaliation-failure

  You charge in too hard, lose your footing, and hit the floor. The teacher removes both players from the game.  @change stat health -3  @change stat stress 3  @change grade physical_education -2

  @next -> @return

  @passage rough-taylor-cared

